"The surah begins with the statement, \"Nay, I swear by the Day of Resurrection.\" The particle \"la\" here before the oath is not for negation, but for emphasis; it signifies the greatness of the matter being sworn by. Most scholars consider this \"la\" to be a silā (emphatic particle), though some state that when it occurs at the beginning of speech, it denotes negation. In that case, it carries a meaning like \"nay, by Allah\" and enhances the significance of the oath. Swearing by the Day of Resurrection and the self-reproaching soul emphasizes the certainty of these realities.\n\nMan thinks that We cannot assemble his bones. Yet Allah is able to gather them together; He can even restore his very fingertips. This points to the subtleties in the creation of man. Man wishes to continue sinning and mockingly asks about the Day of Resurrection. When that day arrives, the sight is dazed, the moon is eclipsed, and the sun and the moon are joined together. In horror, man says, \"Where is the place to flee?\" but there is no refuge; on that day, the final destination is to your Lord alone.\n\nOn that day, man will be informed of everything he sent forward and kept back. Because man is a clear witness (basirah) against himself, he knows in his conscience what he has done. Even if he pours out his excuses, he is a witness against his own self. This describes the true inner nature of man and his condition on the Day of Reckoning.\n\nAddressing the Prophet (peace be upon him), it is stated: \"Move not your tongue therewith to make haste with it.\" For the collecting and reciting of the Quran belongs to Allah. When it is recited, it should be followed, and its explanation should be left to Allah. This is an instruction for the preservation and comprehension of revelation.\n\nPeople love the immediate world and leave the Hereafter. On that day, some faces will be radiant, looking toward their Lord; and some faces will be gloomy, expecting a back-breaking calamity. Then, the moment of death is depicted: the soul reaches the collarbones, and it is said, \"Who can cure him?\" The person realizes the parting, leg intertwines with leg, and on that day the drive is to your Lord alone.\n\nIn the concluding verses, the person who did not give charity, did not pray, denied the truth, and turned away is condemned: \"Woe to you, and woe!\" Does man think he will be left aimless? He was created from a drop of sperm, then became an embryo, then was proportioned and made into male and female. Is Allah, Who did this, not able to bring the dead back to life? Indeed, He is surely able.\n\nThe expression \"did not confirm\" in verses 31–33 of Surah al-Qiyamah means, according to most commentators, that he did not believe. However, some scholars such as Abu Hayyan interpreted this word as meaning he did not give charity. According to Elmalılı, the second interpretation is more meaningful because while the immediately following phrase \"denied the truth\" would merely remain an emphasis under the first interpretation, a broader meaning emerges that encompasses both the refusal to give charity and denial of faith. This interpretation is also in harmony with the verses in Surah al-Muddaththir: \"We were not among those who prayed, nor did we feed the poor.\"\n\nThe letter \"fa\" in the verses explains the state of the sinner who indulges in worldly pleasure and questions the Day of Resurrection: That person neither affirmed the future, nor performed any deed for the sake of Allah, nor gave charity, nor prayed. On the contrary, by denying Allah, the Prophet, the Quran, and the Hereafter, he turned away from the truth and abandoned obedience.\n\nFollowing this defiant attitude, that person went to his family strutting in arrogance. Yet, in the end, he will depart from this world and arrive before his Lord. On that day, his face will not smile, his back will be broken, and he will meet his doom. The concise and succinct expressions of the verses strikingly convey these meanings.\n\nThe expression in verses 34–35, \"Woe to you, and woe!\" is a malediction in Arabic, meaning \"serves you right\" or \"may the greatest disaster befall you.\" Although it has been narrated that these verses were revealed concerning Abu Jahl, it is evident that they encompass everyone in a similar condition.\n\nIn verse 36, man's assumption that he will be left aimless is challenged. The following verses, 37–39, prove that this assumption is invalid by reminding that man was actually created from a simple drop of emitted semen. Verse 40 emphasizes that Allah, Who created these, is also capable of bringing the dead back to life. Regarding saying \"Yes\" at the end of the surah, there is a hadith narrated from the Prophet (peace be upon him)."